From 92a2f034ed94ecdf005095e5e645d8904a8403a5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Andrew Hamilton <adhamilt@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 15 Sep 2026 17:45:30 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] docs/grub: Document serial port restrictions under
 lockdown

Document that the serial command with the raw address is restricted
in lockdown mode to avoid misuse by an attacker (except for if the
given address is already associated with a platform provided
device).

Assisted-by: Claude:Opus-5

Signed-off-by: Andrew Hamilton <adhamilt@gmail.com>
Reviewed-by: Leo Sandoval <lsandova@redhat.com>
Part-of: <https://gitlab.freedesktop.org/gnu-grub/grub/-/merge_requests/266>
---
 docs/grub.texi | 32 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 32 insertions(+)

Index: grub.git/docs/grub.texi
===================================================================
--- grub.git.orig/docs/grub.texi
+++ grub.git/docs/grub.texi
@@ -6583,6 +6583,38 @@ Note, valid @var{port} values, excluding
 by listing terminals with @command{terminal_output}, selecting all names
 prefixed by @samp{serial_} and removing that prefix.
 
+When lockdown is enforced (@pxref{Lockdown}) a @var{port} that gives a raw
+I/O or MMIO address is accepted only if a port is already registered at that
+address, which means the platform described that UART. An address nothing is
+registered at is rejected, because nothing in the UART programming sequence
+can tell a real UART from ordinary memory and the configuration supplying the
+address is not authenticated. Matching is on the address, so it does not
+matter how the address is spelled, and the registered port keeps the access
+size the platform gave it.
+
+The ports a platform describes are:
+
+@itemize @bullet
+@item
+the @samp{com@var{N}} legacy UARTs, named by @var{unit} or by their I/O
+address (x86 only)
+@item
+an ACPI SPCR UART, which @command{serial auto} -- or @command{serial} with no
+options at all -- parses the table for (x86 only). Note that
+@option{--port=auto} does @emph{not} select this: @option{--port} parses its
+argument as a number unless it begins with @samp{mmio,} or @samp{pci,}, so
+@samp{auto} there becomes port 0.
+@item
+a UEFI Serial I/O device, named @samp{efi@var{N}}. On platforms with no
+legacy UART and no SPCR support, such as arm64, this is the route to use.
+@item
+an enumerated PCI device, named @samp{pci,XX:XX.X} or by its I/O address
+@end itemize
+
+An SPCR UART is registered only when @command{serial auto} first parses the
+table, so under lockdown it must be reached through @command{serial auto}
+once before it can be named by its address.
+
 Examples:
 @example
 serial --port=0x3f8 --speed=9600
